From: meanaverage <18336095+meanaverage@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 13:07:46 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(egfx): bound the region rectangles of an H.264 frame

An AVC420 metablock counts its region rectangles in 32 bits, and nothing
bounded how many a frame kept: only what they add up to was, against the
surface's area and the 256 MiB readback limit. Non-overlapping one-pixel
regions pass both, at 10 bytes each on the wire, so a server could send
a frame with a million regions in 10 MB. The client decoded and copied
every one, and the web client built a JavaScript object for each, on the
page's thread, before the decoder saw the frame: a million regions held
the page for about a second, a larger PDU longer; with fewer (below
about 100,000), regions far apart were then read back one by one, and
with more, reading the picture failed with a RangeError.

Two limits:

- An AVC420 bitmap stream (each view of AVC444 is one) with more than
  65,535 region rectangles no longer decodes, so decoding the regions
  takes under a megabyte whatever the size of the PDU. The specification
  sets no limit; this one matches the 16-bit counts of the surface
  commands' rectangles and points (SolidFill, SurfaceToSurface,
  CacheToSurface).
- A frame for an external decoder hands out at most 256 regions. With
  more, their bounding box is read back instead, as already for regions
  that add up to more than their surface. Regions say what changed in
  the picture, and servers describe that in a few rectangles: even
  damage tracked in 64x64 tiles, every other tile of a 1920x1080 screen,
  is 255 of them.
